# .cursorrules
# Coding Standards and Architectural Constraints - TrumpetNotes

## 1. Fundamental Paradigms
- Enforce strict Object-Oriented Programming (OOP): encapsulation, typed interfaces, inheritance, single responsibility (SRP), and design patterns (Factory, Strategy, Observer).
- All business logic (parsing, music theory, transposition) MUST reside within dedicated domain classes, separated from UI components.
- Do NOT use third-party backend orchestration (no n8n, no Docker). Exclusively use Firebase Web SDK v10+ with Cloud Firestore and Firebase Auth.

## 2. PWA & Offline-First Constraints
- Strictly require service worker registration (`sw.js`) with Cache-First for static assets and Network-First/Stale-While-Revalidate for user profile requests.
- Firestore MUST be initialized with `enableIndexedDbPersistence()` (or `initializeFirestore` with `persistentLocalCache`).
- All critical user data MUST be readable and writable completely offline.

## 3. Firestore Path Rules
- CRITICAL: Firestore references MUST ALWAYS have an even number of path segments (`collection/document`).
  - Example: `users/{userId}/notes/{noteId}`.
  - Never construct dangling collection references for document reads/writes.
- Implement strictly validated Firestore Security Rules.

## 4. Code Organization & State Management
- `/src/domain/music/`: Pure OOP domain classes (`Note`, `PitchClass`, `Transposer`, `KeyDetector`, `ScaleEngine`).
- `/src/domain/notes/`: Domain models (`SongNote`, `Folder`, `AudioNoteMetadata`).
- `/src/services/firebase/`: Encapsulated repository classes (`FirestoreService`, `AuthService`).
- `/src/services/storage/`: PWA cache controllers and IndexedDB synchronizers.
- State management must follow the Observer/Store pattern using standard TypeScript classes.

## 5. Refactoring Protocol
- When refactoring, you MUST respect the current app structure dictated in the `.cursorrules`. Never break OOP inheritance or offline mechanisms.